# The introductory example program (Example.testProgram in the Arend project).
#
# Arend-authored: prog.ast is `ExamplePrint:exampleSexpr` serialized by
# Serialize.ard and regenerated by test/tools/regen-arend-asts.sh, so compiling
# it exercises the serializer and tools/ast-to-arend as well as the generator.
#
# No expected value: the result is constructor DATA, not a scalar, and our Java
# renderer prints it MULTI-LINE with per-level indentation
#
#   1(
#     1(
#       0
#     )
#   )
#
# which is not comparable to any other backend's rendering (see the deferred
# "output normalization" decision in ../../../README.md). The value is still
# recorded per run.
#
# `xfail`: this program does not survive the .ast round trip, and deliberately
# so. Example.ard's declaration set is the Arend-side FEATURE list -- among the
# reachable definitions it carries `openTerm`, whose body is a free variable, and
# Serialize.ard renders that as the marker `tVar_UNREACHABLE` (λ□ coming out of
# Rocq has no `tVar`, hence the name). `peregrine ast box`, the first half of the
# java generation stage, rejects the file while parsing it:
#
#   could not read type 'term', expected nullary constructor name, one of tBox,
#   found tVar_UNREACHABLE
#
# So the gap is in the PROGRAM, not in the harness: an `.ast` only Peregrine
# cannot read is not a program any backend can run. The row is kept, with the
# reason in `xfail`, because it is the record of that gap; removing `openTerm`
# from Example.ard (or teaching the stage to skip boxing an already-boxed file)
# would turn it green again. The row still reports its real status (`gen-fail`),
# so a DIFFERENT failure here would not hide behind the known one.
expected=
backends=java
runtime=
xfail=prog.ast contains tVar_UNREACHABLE, which `peregrine ast box` cannot parse
note=succ (succ zero)
